# Q-functions, Wronskians, $\det N$ {#sec:q}

**Punchline.** Every eigenstate is a triple of polynomial-times-exponential solutions of one third-order ODE (the oper built from $S_1,S_2,S_3$); the ODE has local exponents $\{0,\lambda_2+1,\lambda_1+2\}$ at each $\theta_s$, which forces linear relations among the jets of the solutions and fixes the Frobenius constant $\kappa_s=a_s/c_2$. The overlap of two states is detected by a $6\times6$ determinant of residue brackets whose columns come from a Lagrange identity.

## The oper and its Q-functions {#sec:oper}

The dual oper is
$$
\bigl(\partial^3-S_1\partial^2+S_2\partial-S_3\bigr)P=0,\qquad P^j=e^{\zeta_ju}\,p_j(u),\ \ \deg p_j=\mu_j,\ p_j\ \text{monic},\label{eq:oper}
$$
where $\mu$ is the weight of the state. For each of the three exponentials there is exactly one polynomial solution (kernel dimension 1), and $\sum\deg=|\mu|$. The XXX dual Baxter equation becomes exactly this oper at order $h^3$, with $Q^j=z_j^{u/h}q^j\to e^{\zeta_ju}p_j$.^[Agents P1 (`RESULTS-Qside.md` Q1; all 64 states of $[2,1,0]$, ODE residual $3.9\cdot10^{-55}$) and P2 (`RESULTS-xxx-limit.md` X4, sympy, symbolic $h$).]

The direct Q-functions are a little subtler, and this was our first surprise. One expects them to solve the formal adjoint of (\ref{eq:oper}). They do not: the plain adjoint has \emph{no} solutions of the form $e^{-\zeta u}\times$polynomial. What is true is that $\psi_i=\Pi^{-p}Q_i$ solves the adjoint, with $\Pi(u)=(u-\theta_1)(u-\theta_2)$,
$$
Q_i=e^{-\zeta_iu}q_i,\quad \deg q_i=2\lambda_1-\mu_i,\qquad \text{direct oper}=\Pi^{p}\circ(\text{formal adjoint})\circ\Pi^{-p},\ \ p=\lambda_1-\lambda_3.\label{eq:direct}
$$
The XXX Baxter equation at order $h^3$ gives the adjoint acting on $Q_i/\nu^2$, which is the same statement for the adjoint. For $[2,1,0]$ the gauge power $2$ is forced by the degrees; for $[3,1,0]$ and $[3,2,0]$ a scan over $p=0,\dots,5$ gives no solution at $p=0,1,2$, exactly the right degrees $6-\mu_i$ at $p=3$ in all 36 sectors, and degrees raised by $2(p-3)$ above. All our data have $\lambda_3=0$, so $p=\lambda_1-\lambda_3$ and $p=\lambda_1$ are not separated by data; the exponent argument below selects the former.^[Agents P1 (gauge $2$, `RESULTS-Qside.md` section 0), P3 (`RESULTS-Qside-310.md` Q1, gauge scan table), P4 (`RESULTS-Qside-320.md` Q1), P2 (X4: the direct Baxter equation is $\nu^2\circ$adjoint$\circ\nu^{-2}$ exactly). Wrong-sign and wrong-gauge controls have no solutions to degree 8--10.] Table~\ref{tab:degrees} lists the degrees for the three representations at the top state.

